  /* ------------------------------------------- Al Brooks Gaps 1 */
  var gaps1 = {
    id: 'gaps-one',
    navGroup: 'Al Brooks 课程',
    navLabel: '缺口课 1',
    title: '缺口课 1 · 定义与分类',
    en: 'Gaps 1',
    eyebrow: 'Brooks 课程 01',
    accent: '#2563eb',
    tagline: 'Brooks 对缺口的定义比任何教科书都宽：支撑与阻力之间的任何空隙，都是缺口。',
    plainTitle: '缺口课 1',
    tags: ["缺口定义","传统分类","岛形反转","momentum","测量移动"],
    chapters: [
      { id: 'definition', label: '他的定义：支撑与阻力之间的任何空隙' },
      { id: 'traditional', label: '传统分类：为什么全都只能事后确认' },
      { id: 'island', label: '岛形顶 / 底：他把标准放宽了' },
      { id: 'momentum', label: '核心信条：缺口 = 趋势棒 = 突破 = 高潮' },
      { id: 'chart', label: '图表上有没有缺口，不影响含义' },
      { id: 'measured-move', label: '怎么从缺口量目标' },
      { id: 'bar-count', label: '判断"测量还是竭尽"：数趋势已经走了多少根棒' },
      { id: 'micro', label: '别忽略小缺口' },
      { id: 'transcript', label: '本集英文原文（完整保留）' },
    ],
    body: [
      '<p class="lede">本节整理自 Brooks Trading Course《Gaps》四集的第一集。Brooks 在这一集亲自讲了两件最容易被忽略的事：<strong>他对"缺口"的定义比任何教科书都宽</strong>，以及<strong>所有传统缺口分类——突破、测量、竭尽、岛形——都只有在事后才能确认</strong>。文末附本集英文原文（完整保留，未做删节）。</p>\n\n<h2 id="definition">1. 他的定义：支撑与阻力之间的任何空隙</h2>\n\n教科书只看"跳空开盘"，Brooks 的定义是——**缺口 = 当前价格与任何支撑或阻力之间的那段空间**。支撑或阻力可以是：\n\n- 任意一根 K 线的**高 / 低 / 开 / 收**（当前棒或之前的棒都算）\n- 任意一条**均线**（哪条均线不重要）\n- **趋势线**、**趋势通道线**\n\n很多时候这个支撑或阻力本身很微小，但由此形成的缺口却很重要。\n\n<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>位置</th><th>为什么算缺口</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th class="rowhead">本棒收盘 vs 前高</th><td>收盘略高于前一根棒的高点，中间有空间</td></tr><tr><th class="rowhead">本棒低点 vs 前高</th><td>低点没有跌破前一根棒的高点，中间有空间</td></tr><tr><th class="rowhead">本棒低点 vs 均线</th><td>两者之间有空间，就是缺口</td></tr><tr><th class="rowhead">本棒开盘 vs 前一根收盘</th><td>开盘略高于前收，也是缺口</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<div class="callout insight"><div class="callout-body"><p class="callout-title">别只盯着肉眼可见的跳空</p><p>Brooks 的缺口绝大多数是<strong>肉眼看不见的微小空间</strong>。他明确说过："大多数交易者根本没意识到它们是缺口，但它们有预测价值。"这一节的重点不是记住定义，而是<strong>把"找缺口"变成扫描支撑阻力之间的一切空档</strong>。</p></div></div>\n\n<h2 id="traditional">2. 传统分类：为什么全都只能事后确认</h2>\n\n教科书把缺口分成突破、测量（中继）、竭尽、普通、岛形几类，并给出各自的含义。Brooks 的评价很直接：**这些分类全都是事后叙事，当时你永远不知道它是哪一种。**\n\n<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>类型</th><th>教科书说法</th><th>Brooks 的实际看法</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th class="rowhead">突破缺口</th><td>趋势起点的跳空</td><td>只有事后才知道。当时还在区间里、缺口遍地都是，它完全可能在一两天内反转，变成又一个无意义的缺口</td></tr><tr><th class="rowhead">测量缺口</th><td>趋势中途，预示等幅目标</td><td>同样只有事后才知道——只有当市场在近乎完美的测量目标位反转时，你才回头说"哦那是个测量缺口"</td></tr><tr><th class="rowhead">竭尽缺口</th><td>趋势末端，随后被回补</td><td>缺口<strong>被回补之后</strong>才回溯性地称为竭尽。而且<strong>竭尽 ≠ 反转</strong>，只代表一方力竭</td></tr><tr><th class="rowhead">普通缺口</th><td>区间内最常见的缺口</td><td>无预测价值。区间里的大多数突破最终都会反转</td></tr><tr><th class="rowhead">岛形反转</th><td>两个缺口同水平、中间无成交</td><td>Brooks 大幅放宽标准，见下节</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n关于竭尽缺口，有两个细节必须记住：\n\n- **竭尽不等于趋势反转。** 它只代表买方（或卖方）力竭，通常要等几根棒才重新进场。\n- **多数情况下竭尽 = 转化为交易区间**，而不是立刻反转成反向趋势。可能最终会反转，但通常只是"跌势停止"。\n\n<h2 id="island">3. 岛形顶 / 底：他把标准放宽了</h2>\n\n传统定义很严格：两个缺口必须在**同一价格水平**，且中间**没有任何成交**。Brooks 的放宽版本：\n\n- 只要**跳空上去、再跳空下来** → 就是岛形顶（反过来即岛形底）\n- **不在乎两个缺口是否同一水平**（教科书要求严格对齐，他明确说不在乎）\n- 岛可以是 **1 根棒，也可以是 10 / 20 / 30 / 40 / 50 根棒**\n- 甚至可以把"趋势末端的竭尽缺口 + 随后向上的跳空"这**整段**称为一个岛形底\n\n<div class="callout warn"><div class="callout-body"><p class="callout-title">但岛形并不比其他反转更重要</p><p>这是 Brooks 最关键的态度之一：<strong>"这个到底是岛形底还是岛形顶，真的重要吗？不重要。它只是一次反转，并不比其他任何反转更重要。"</strong>一旦出现一连串强趋势棒，你根本不在乎那里有没有缺口——市场在反转，这就够了。<strong>大多数反转会失败，所以大多数岛形不会演变成趋势。</strong></p></div></div>\n\n<h2 id="momentum">4. 核心信条：缺口 = 趋势棒 = 突破 = 高潮</h2>\n\n**"我不在乎它是岛形顶、岛形底、竭尽缺口还是突破缺口。我只在乎 momentum（动能）。对我来说，每根趋势棒都是突破；每根趋势棒都是缺口（哪怕你看不到实际的空隙）；每根趋势棒都是高潮。"**\n\n- **10 根带很多缺口的多头棒** vs **1 根没有任何缺口的多头棒** —— 在他眼里没有区别\n- 任何趋势棒、任何一连串趋势棒，同时是：**突破 + 高潮 + 缺口**\n- 反过来也成立：跌破通道下轨的大空头棒（楔形 1-2-3 之后）= 趋势棒 = 突破，通常也伴随缺口\n\n<h2 id="chart">5. 图表上有没有缺口，不影响含义</h2>\n\nBrooks 给了两个对照例子，用来说明"缺口只是显示方式的差异"：\n\n<div class="concept-grid"><div class="concept"><p class="concept-term">E-mini 期货 vs 现货指数</p><span class="concept-en">Futures vs Cash Index</span><p>同一段行情：E-mini 是<strong>跳空开盘</strong>（有明显缺口），现货指数只是<strong>两根大阳棒</strong>（没有缺口）。含义完全相同——都是"市场认定价格错了，必须快速移动"。</p></div><div class="concept"><p class="concept-term">EUR/USD vs FXE</p><span class="concept-en">Spot vs Low-volume ETF</span><p>走势基本一致，唯一差别是<strong>FXE 图上有缺口</strong>——因为当时 FXE 成交量不大。<strong>成交量小的品种缺口多，成交量大的品种缺口少</strong>，但对应趋势棒的含义完全一样。</p></div></div>\n\n<h2 id="measured-move">6. 怎么从缺口量目标</h2>\n\n有缺口，就可以量等幅目标（Measured Move）。Brooks 用了几种基准，**事先不知道该用哪个，通常几个都标出来**：\n\n1. **缺口中点**（他最常用）\n2. **缺口的上沿 / 下沿**\n3. 突破第一根棒的**开盘** → 最后一根棒的**收盘**（两棒突破时）\n4. 从**趋势起点**到**突破棒高点**的高度\n\n<div class="callout tip"><div class="callout-body"><p class="callout-title">什么情况下更可能走出趋势</p><p>同时满足越多，跟进概率越高：<strong>棒体很大</strong>、<strong>收盘远高于左侧大量棒（20~30 根以上）</strong>、<strong>收盘接近自己的极值</strong>、<strong>收盘站上大量阻力</strong>（例如一根长期下降趋势线）。另外，突破棒大概率<strong>至少走两腿</strong>——顺势方买第一次回踩，押注突破失败的一方在回踩时认亏回补。</p></div></div>\n\n止盈行为也有规律：市场到达测量目标时开始分批止盈，**目标位下方出现长影线**就说明已在目标下方获利了结。看到「缺口 → 缺口被测试 → 市场试图反转向上」，大概率会走到测量目标。\n\n<h2 id="bar-count">7. 判断"测量还是竭尽"：数趋势已经走了多少根棒</h2>\n\n这是本集最实用的判断框架——既然分类只能事后确认，那当时靠什么？**靠这根大棒出现在趋势的第几根。**\n\n<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>大棒出现的位置</th><th>更可能的性质</th><th>说明</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th class="rowhead">约 10 根棒</th><td>测量缺口</td><td>更可能是新一腿起点。但趋势本身还不强，缺口可能被回补、趋势结束转入区间</td></tr><tr><th class="rowhead">20–30 根棒</th><td>两者皆可能</td><td>必须同时防备竭尽，不能只押一边</td></tr><tr><th class="rowhead">40–50 根棒，且是最大的那根</th><td>竭尽末端</td><td>通常是趋势的竭尽末端，而不是新一腿的起点</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<div class="callout danger"><div class="callout-body"><p class="callout-title">这是概率，不是必然</p><p>Brooks 自己给了反例：一根出现在下跌趋势 40~50 根棒处、而且是<strong>最大的一根空头趋势棒</strong>，直觉上该是竭尽，但<strong>它最终仍成了测量缺口</strong>，市场继续走到测量目标才止盈。所以：<strong>看到大棒 + 跟进棒，大概率至少还有一腿，但要用后续棒验证，不要当成铁律。</strong></p></div></div>\n\n<h2 id="micro">8. 别忽略小缺口</h2>\n\n一根棒的**收盘**与那根棒的**高点**之间的微小空间，也是缺口。不熟悉的人很容易忽略，但它们**很重要**，尤其是**连续出现好几个**的时候——连续的小缺口就是**一方强劲**的标志。\n\n<div class="callout insight"><div class="callout-body"><p class="callout-title">这一集的收尾两句</p><p><strong>"我用『缺口』这个词的方式和几乎所有人都不同。我用的是对交易者有用的方式：缺口就是当前价格与某个支撑或阻力之间的空间。"</strong><br><strong>"大多数缺口没什么意义，但有很多缺口——大多数交易者根本没意识到它们是缺口——是有预测价值的。它们能帮你判断：卖压是否足够强、市场是否要继续下跌；买压是否足够强、市场是否要继续上涨。"</strong></p></div></div>\n\n<h2 id="transcript">9. 本集英文原文（完整保留）</h2>\n\n<details class="tscript"><summary>Brooks Trading Course — Gaps, Video 1 of 4 <span class="tscript-note">（英文校正稿，仅修正语音识别错误与标点，内容未删节）</span></summary><div class="tscript-body">\n<h4>Opening — a very broad definition</h4>\n<p>This is Al Brooks. Thank you for watching the Brooks Trading Course. This is the first of four videos on gaps.</p>\n<p>I want to begin by what I mean when I talk about a gap. I use a very broad definition. For me, a gap is a space — it is the space between the current price and any support or resistance. For example, support or resistance can be the high, low, open, or close of any bar, the current bar or prior bars; any moving average; trend lines; trend channel lines. A lot of times the support or resistance can be minor, yet it turns out that the gap is important.</p>\n<p>At the close of this bar, there is space between its high and a prior high — its high and the high of this lower high, and the high of the prior bar. Those are all gaps. For example, this is a gap between this close and that high. The close of this bar is slightly above the high of this bar — a gap between this close and these highs as well. The low of this bar did not fall below the high of this bar, and therefore there is a gap here. Here is a moving average — it does not matter what moving average — there is a space between the low of this bar and the moving average; that is therefore a gap. There is a space between the low of this bar and that high, so that is a gap. There is a space between this low and that high, and therefore another gap. Look at the open of this bar and the close of the prior bar: this open is slightly above that close, and therefore there is a gap.</p>\n<h4>Exhaustion gap</h4>\n<p>Exhaustion gap: a big trend bar late in a bull trend. Usually the gap will close — the gap between the close of the big bull trend bar, which will end up as the exhaustion bar, and the high of the prior bar. And you can see we traded down below that high; it closed the gap, and that made this bar an exhaustion gap bar. Exhaustion gap does not mean trend reversal; it just means the bulls are exhausted, and they usually wait a few bars before buying again, which they did. This is a buy signal bar and the bulls bought. This is a test of that buy signal bar high, and there is a gap between the low of this bar and the high of that bar.</p>\n<h4>Traditional gaps</h4>\n<p>I want to talk a little bit about traditional gaps. For example, this is a daily chart — does not matter what market, but it is a daily chart. You can see that the low of this big bull trend bar did not fall below the high of the prior bar; that is a traditional gap — between the low of a bar and the high of the prior bar, that is a gap up. A gap down is the space between the high of a bar and the low of the prior bar right here. A gap up is a sign of strong bulls. Here is a gap down, and it is a sign of strong bears. The market actually opened down here, rallied strongly, was unable to get back above yesterday low, and then sold off to a new low later in the day — and this is the gap down.</p>\n<p>On daily charts, gaps are common. These green boxes are gaps up and the red boxes are gaps down. They usually do not have much significance. They are less common on weekly charts and extremely uncommon on monthly charts, except in very thinly traded markets. They are also less common on forex charts, although you sometimes see gaps on the forex charts as well. On five-minute charts of very liquid markets — markets that have a lot of volume — gaps are rare.</p>\n<p>Gaps on the daily charts of stocks are especially common in trading ranges, and they are usually meaningless in trading ranges; they do not have any predictive value. Most breakouts in trading ranges ultimately reverse, and therefore a gap in a trading range has no predictive value. We have a gap up, a gap down, a gap up, a gap up again — no predictive value, they are meaningless.</p>\n<h4>Breakaway gap</h4>\n<p>Again, a daily chart — we are rallying, and the next day the low stayed above the high of this bar, and it happened after a bear trend. This is therefore called a breakaway gap or a breakout gap. You know in hindsight that that is what it is: if you have a trend up after a gap like that, you look back and say, wow, that is a breakaway gap, the start of a bull trend.</p>\n<p>A gap up early in a bull trend — at the time it happens, you think it might be that way, but we are still in a trading range, and we have lots of gaps in a trading range, and this could easily, quickly reverse within a day or two or three, and then be just another meaningless gap in the trading range, like all of these other gaps were.</p>\n<h4>Measuring gap</h4>\n<p>We sold off, we have a gap down, we have a gap down. Whenever you have gaps, you are always going to be thinking that they could lead to measured moves. Only in hindsight do you know that this was a measuring gap — when you see the market reverse up at just about a perfect measured move target, you know this was a measuring gap. At the time it happened, you wondered if it might be a measuring gap.</p>\n<p>Here we have a double top with this high and a reversal down; that means the gap is important, and maybe it will fall for a measured move. And when it got down to the measured move target, the bulls bought to get long — higher low, major trend reversal; bear trend, bull breakout, higher low — and the bears bought to buy back their shorts.</p>\n<p>When you have a gap late in a trend, it often closes, as it did here. This went above the low of that bar, and then traders would call it an exhaustion gap. At the time that it happened, you did not know if it was going to be a measuring gap leading to a measured move down — it pretty much did. From here to here, it is almost a measured move down. Once it closes, traders begin to wonder if it is an exhaustion move. This final gap was the last move by the bears before the bear trend ended and converted into a trading range.</p>\n<h4>Island bottoms and tops</h4>\n<p>And then here we have a gap up — this was the start of a rally. You could also call this entire thing an island bottom: we have a gap down, a trading range, and then a gap up. Traditional island tops and bottoms: there is no trading that takes place between the two.</p>\n<p>Here we traded repeatedly around this gap; in my mind, we sold off, we rallied strongly — this is a reversal. We had a gap down at the end of the sell-off and a gap up at the beginning of the rally. I would call all of this an island bottom, even though it is not a conventional island bottom. This is a conventional island bottom: we have a gap down and a gap up, and there is no trading in between.</p>\n<p>Once the market moves above the top of the gap, and the gap is late in a trend, traders would call this a closed gap, and they would call it an exhaustion gap. Lots of other gaps are common, especially in trading ranges, and traders call them gaps or common gaps.</p>\n<p>On this slide I am talking about the traditional definitions of gaps. As I said on the prior slide, I have a much broader definition of a gap, and mine, I think, is much better for traders.</p>\n<p>I mentioned island tops and bottoms. If the market gaps up and then gaps down, it is an island top. Traditional trading: you want the two gaps to be at the same level, and you do not want any trading taking place in the gap up before you get the gap down. But for me, if it gaps up and then down, it is an island top.</p>\n<p>This is a bigger island top: gap up and gap down. We gapped down, gapped down again, and gapped up — this is an island bottom. If you want, you can call these two bars an island bottom. Island bottoms sometimes lead to big trends. Here we get an island top and a big sell-off; an island bottom and a big rally.</p>\n<p>But most gaps are meaningless. Does it matter that this is an island bottom or that this is an island top? No — it is simply a reversal, and it is no more important than any other reversal.</p>\n<p>When you see it happening, you are wondering if it is the start of a swing here; you wonder if it is the start of a trend up, but you do not know until after you get a series of strong bull bars. And at that point you do not care if there are gaps or just two or three big bull bars. You know the market is reversing up, so the gap really adds nothing to what you are doing as a trader. It is simply a reversal: we sold off, we rallied, the market is reversing, and therefore traders are looking to buy.</p>\n<p>Some charts rarely have gaps. When that is the case, you look at every reversal as a variation of an island top or bottom. It does not matter that we have a gap down, another gap down, a gap up — all that matters is the market went down and now it went up, whether or not there are gaps. Most reversals fail, and therefore most island bottoms and most island tops do not lead to trends. Here we have an island bottom, and you can see we closed the gap over here, and therefore the island bottom failed.</p>\n<p>I mentioned that the gaps do not have to be at exactly the same level if you talk about islands, although a lot of the textbooks will say that the gaps have to be exactly the same. Well, here we have a gap up and a gap down — to me this is an island top; I do not care that this gap is far below that gap.</p>\n<p>Here this is more of a traditional island bottom: we have a gap down and a gap up, and there is a space — you can draw a line right between the two gaps, and there is no trading taking place. This is a traditional island bottom. It is no more important than any other type of island top or island bottom, and you can see it resulted in a trading range and not a bull trend.</p>\n<p>For me, island bottom: we have a gap down, a gap up. When I see that, I think that this could be the start of a bull trend, and it makes me get ready to buy if I have not bought already. But again, it is no more important than any big reversal, whether or not there is a gap.</p>\n<p>A two-bar island bottom — you can also call this whole thing an island bottom: gap down and then gap up, or a gap up, so island bottom with these two gaps; island bottom with this gap and that gap; island bottom with this gap down and that gap up. An island can be a single bar, or it could be ten, twenty, thirty, forty, fifty bars.</p>\n<p>For example, we gapped down, we gapped up — this is an island bottom; it resulted in a trading range. This entire thing I mentioned earlier is an island bottom: we have a gap down at the end of a trend — an exhaustion gap — and we have a gap up here, and it led to a bull trend. Therefore I would call this whole thing an island bottom. I would not consider that particularly important. What is more important to me is: we sold off, we reversed up — it is a reversal. I do not need to know that it is an island bottom for me to want to buy.</p>\n<p>A lot of times, when you have island bottoms and tops, there are many gaps to look at and many ways to draw them. For example, this whole thing is an island bottom, but within it there are other island tops and island bottoms.</p>\n<h4>The core: all I care about is momentum</h4>\n<p>I said that I do not care if there is an island top or an island bottom or an exhaustion gap or a breakout gap. All I care about is momentum. For me, every trend bar is a breakout; every trend bar is a gap, even though you do not see an actual space; every trend bar is a climax.</p>\n<p>Here I talked about this: there is a gap between this close and that low; a gap between this close and any of these lows; a gap between this close and that low. Those are all gaps. Here there is a gap between this low and that high — this low tested this high, so it tested the gap.</p>\n<p>Here we have a very strong series of bull bars. In my mind, it is no different from ten bull bars with a lot of gaps in it, or one bull bar with no gaps. Any bull bar, any series of bull bars, is a breakout; it is a climax, and it is a gap. Gap between this close and this high; gap between this close and this high; gap between this close and that high. Strong bull breakout.</p>\n<p>Every bull trend bar, every series of bull trend bars — I think of them exactly the same as I do a gap. It is also a breakout; it is also a climax. But a climax can go a long way before there is a pause. This was a buy climax, and it turned out to be the end of a swing up; we traded down for about forty bars.</p>\n<p>We have a big bear reversal breaking below the bottom of this channel — a wedge, one, two, three. It is a trend bar, and therefore it is a breakout. And there is usually a gap associated with it if it is the start of a bear trend — for example, a gap between the close of this bar and the low of that bar, a gap between the high of this pullback bar and the low of that bar.</p>\n<p>Sometimes you get these really small gaps — for example, the close of this bar and the high of that bar. If you are not familiar with these things, it is easy to ignore them, but they are important, especially if you get several of them. They are a sign of strong bulls.</p>\n<p>Big bear breakout below this low, below all of these lows, but immediately reversed up. The gap between this close and that low closed over here, and therefore this bear breakout, this bear trend bar, is also an exhaustion gap.</p>\n<h4>Measured moves from gaps</h4>\n<p>We have a bull breakout: the low of this bar is above the high of these bars — there is a gap. Therefore traders will look at the bottom of the gap for a possible measured move up, and they will look to take profits around the measured move target. This theoretically could have been a double top: we could have rallied here and sold off; I look at it as a potential double top. If it did reverse down, traders would look at the neckline for a measured move down, just like the height of this would have led to a measured move down because of a double top. If we break above it, I look for a measured move up based upon the height of this sell-off. Every gap is a trend bar; every trend bar is a gap.</p>\n<p>This is the E-mini chart; it gapped up on the open. This is the cash index chart. The cash index often does not have gaps. If you are looking at the cash index, you would say, wow, that is a pretty big two-bar breakout. However, if you are trading the futures contract, you have a big gap opening. And to me, it is the same. I do not care if it is two big trend bars; I do not care if it is a gap — they are the same. It is a quick move up; the market is deciding that the price is wrong and has to move quickly. Whether this is a gap or a trend bar, or a series of trend bars, it does not matter — they are telling me the same thing: the market wants to go higher.</p>\n<p>This is the gap opening on the one-minute futures chart, and then this is the big bull trend bar on the cash index chart.</p>\n<p>This is the euro versus the dollar five-minute chart, and this is FXE — it is an ETF that tracks the euro — and you can see they are doing basically the same thing. One big difference is we have gaps on this chart. At the time I made this chart, the volume was not particularly heavy in FXE, and whenever a chart does not have a lot of volume, it tends to have a lot of gaps.</p>\n<p>This gap down corresponds to this bear bar; this gap down corresponds to this bear bar; we have a gap down here and a bear bar — that gap and bear bar corresponds to this bear bar. If I am just looking at the euro, I am not seeing the gaps; if I am just looking at FXE, I am seeing gaps. And they mean exactly the same thing, whether or not you see gaps, or whether you simply see trend bars. A trend bar is a gap; a gap is a trend bar, and I trade them exactly the same way.</p>\n<h4>Reading the breakout bar</h4>\n<p>We have a very big bull trend bar closing far above the high of the prior bar and far above the high of the past twenty or thirty bars. Therefore there is an increased chance that it is the start of a bull trend. There is a gap between this close and this high, that high, and this high. It is probably a breakout gap, and it is probably also a measuring gap. The low of this bar stayed above this high and above that high. This space is probably going to lead to a measured move up — from this low to this high — and the market will probably go up for a measured move.</p>\n<p>A breakout bar: you can call it a bull breakout; you can call it a gap, because there is a gap between this close and these highs.</p>\n<p>When the bar is big like this, and it closes far above a lot of bars to the left, and it closes near its high, it is more likely going to be the start of a move up. Chances are the move up will have at least two legs: bulls will buy the first pullback, and the bears who sold, hoping the breakout would fail, will use the first pullback to buy back their shorts at a loss.</p>\n<p>A breakout bar, a gap bar, has a higher probability of leading to a trend up if it closes above a lot of resistance — for example, if there was a big bear trend line coming down here. Here, closing far above a lot of prior highs — that is significant; that increases the chances that you are going to get follow-through buying. Gaps between this close and the high of the bar before it, and above any of the prior highs — those are all gaps. This close is above that high, and therefore this is a gap as well — another sign of strong bulls.</p>\n<p>I would call these two bull bars a breakout — a two-bar bull breakout if you want; you can also call it a gap.</p>\n<p>This bull bar became a measuring gap, although you cannot see it on this chart. The high — we got a measured move up from this low to right around here, the middle of that bull bar.</p>\n<h4>Reading the bear breakout</h4>\n<p>We have a breakout — the start of a bear trend. A bear breakout: this close is a little bit below that low, maybe not, maybe around the same price as a tail. But here we have a bigger bear bar closing on its low or near its low, and well below the low of this bar. These three bear bars reversed this rally, beginning a bear reversal, and three consecutive bear bars closing below their midpoints — one closing on its low, two having good bear bodies — we are probably going lower.</p>\n<p>There is a bull trend line here, and we are breaking below the bull trend line. The bigger the bar is, the more it closes on its low, the more bars to the left it closes below, the more likely we are going to go lower.</p>\n<p>It closed below the trend line, so there is a gap between its close and the trend line, a gap between its close and the low of the prior bar. Gap here, and we have an even bigger bear breakout, and there is a gap between its close and the low of the prior bar — its close, this low; its close, that low.</p>\n<p>We have a four-bar bear breakout. If you want, you can call it a gap; you can call it a breakout; you can call it a reversal. Strong enough momentum so that there is going to be at least a second leg down.</p>\n<p>Two big bear bars closing on or near their lows, breaking far below the trend line — you could call these two bars alone a breakout or a gap. At a minimum, traders will look for a measured move based upon the open of the first bar of the breakout to the close of the final bar of the breakout. Here it is a two-bar breakout: the market is probably going to fall for a measured move from this open to that close, so somewhere down here.</p>\n<h4>Taking profits at the measured move target</h4>\n<p>We have a breakout: a close well above this high, a very big bull bar closing on its high. The bears tried to get a reversal, but this low stayed above the breakout point. We have a gap and then a bull close — it looks like we are going higher; we are probably going up for a measured move. Maybe simply from this low to that high, probably from the start of the bull trend to that high. This is probably the middle of a move; bulls will look to take partial profits at around a measured move up.</p>\n<p>The measured move can be from this low to this high, or from this low to the middle of that gap — you do not know in advance. This gap is very small, so the target would be about the same whichever price you use for your measured move up. This will be the target, and you can see when the market got there, bulls started to take profits. This tail means bulls started to take profits right below the target, and then here they clearly took partial or full profits.</p>\n<p>When you see a gap like this, and you see it get tested, and you see the market is trying to reverse up, it is probably going to reach the measured move target — so traders will look to buy and then take partial profits or full profits at the target.</p>\n<p>Big bear bar closing on its low, closing below the low of the past prior bars — possible measuring gap. The bulls tried to reverse the bear breakout but failed. We closed down below the midpoint of this bar, so we have a bear follow-through bar. That increases the chances that we are going lower; it also increases the chances that this will be a measuring gap between this high and that low.</p>\n<p>Traders will look for a measured move down from the top of the bear trend here to the top of the gap here, or from the middle of the gap here. The gap in this case is the high of this bar and the low of that bar, but it could be the high of a bar two or three bars later, or the low of a bar two or three, or even five or ten bars earlier. Top of the bear trend, the middle of the gap — this would be a measured move target. Some computers will use this low, and the target would be a little bit higher. Bears sold and took profits around the measured move projection.</p>\n<h4>Measuring gap or exhaustion? Count the bars</h4>\n<p>Every bull trend bar is a gap. If you get a big bull trend bar late in a bull trend — usually twenty or thirty bars into the bull trend, but sometimes as early as ten bars into the bull trend — you have to wonder if it is an exhaustion end of the trend instead of a measuring gap and the start of a strong leg up.</p>\n<p>At this point we have a gap — this low, that high. This might be a measuring gap; it is not a particularly big bar, but it still could lead to a measured move up. However, we are ten bars into the trend; the trend is not all that strong. You have to be thinking that it is possible the gap will close, and the bull trend will end, and the market will enter a trading range or possibly even a bear trend.</p>\n<p>The gap is between two prices: the low of this bar, which is the low of the pullback bar, and the high of this bar, which is the breakout point — and the gap is the space in between. The bottom of the bull trend is here; traders will look for a measured move up either based upon the middle of the gap or from the high of this bar. They will look to take profits around a measured move up.</p>\n<p>We have a bull bar closing above the high of this bar, and therefore it is a gap, but now we are thirty bars into a bull trend and we are near a measured move target. This is more likely going to be an exhaustion gap, and it will close. More likely it will be an exhaustion gap than another measuring gap — and you can see the gap closed a couple bars later. The bull trend ended and converted into a trading range. It might resume again, but a gap — a big bull trend bar, in fact the biggest bull trend bar in probably twenty bars — usually is an exhaustion end of the trend and not the start of a new leg up.</p>\n<p>Euro versus the dollar five-minute chart. This is the biggest bear trend bar in the trend, and it is occurring forty or fifty bars into the trend. It could be a measuring gap in the middle of a bear trend, but whenever you get the biggest bear trend bar occurring twenty, thirty, forty bars into a trend, you have got to be thinking that it might be an exhaustion end of the trend and not the start of an even stronger leg down.</p>\n<p>When this bar closed, there are many ways to look at the gap — maybe from the close of this bar to any of these lows, certainly to this breakout point. And here, for the bears, they are hoping this is a pullback and the bear trend will continue down; the bulls are hoping that it is a failed bear breakout, and that this becomes an exhaustion gap, and that the market rallies and closes the gap. Usually it tries to get at least a little bit above the top of the final sell climax. The middle of the gap is here; possibly a measured move down from the middle of the gap, possibly a measured move down from this high to that low — we are already at that target. If it reaches one target, then you look for another — for example, the middle of the gap.</p>\n<p>Even though it is the biggest bear trend bar, thirty or forty bars into a bear trend, it ended up as a measuring gap, and you can see when the market got down to the measured move, traders took profits. The bulls were not able to get the market to go above that bull bar. The probability, after you see a very big bear bar and a bear follow-through bar, is that you are going to get at least a small second leg down, possibly a measured move down.</p>\n<p>Another big bear bar occurring about fifteen or twenty bars after this one — another follow-through bar. Gaps from this close to that low, and this low and this low — all breakout points. But the gaps ultimately closed, and this was an exhaustion gap late in a bear trend. An exhaustion sell climax does not mean a reversal into a bull trend; most of the time it means conversion into a trading range. It might ultimately reverse, but usually an exhaustion move means the bears are exhausted and they will stop selling as aggressively and the market stops going down — not necessarily reverses into a bull trend. Once the gap closed, it became an exhaustion gap.</p>\n<h4>Closing</h4>\n<p>I use the term gap differently from just about anyone else. I use it in a way that is useful for traders: a gap is simply the space between the current price and some support or resistance. Most gaps do not mean much, but there are a lot of gaps that I will talk about in the next several videos that most traders are not even aware are gaps, yet they have predictive value. They help let you know if there is good selling pressure and the market is going lower, or if there is good buying pressure and the market is going higher.</p>\n<p>I am Al Brooks. Thank you for watching the Brooks Trading Course. This is the first of four videos on gaps.</p>\n</div></details>',
    ].join(''),
  };

